      "side": 1,
      "message": "Hi Frank -- I went through the other FSALs:\n\n- GPFS crosses into nested GPFS file systems the same way VFS does, so it now\n  uses the mount point inodes Ganesha records when it reads the mount table.\n  I can\u0027t run GPFS here, so that part is only build-tested.\n- PROXY_V4 now asks the backend server for mounted_on_fileid and passes it\n  through, and uses fileid if the backend doesn\u0027t return it. Decoding\n  mounted_on_fileid used to fail with NFS4ERR_ATTRNOTSUPP, so nothing could\n  ask for it before. I tested it with a Ganesha VFS backend that has a file\n  system mounted inside the export: through the old PROXY_V4 a Linux client\n  saw the nested root with the same inode as the export root, and now it sees\n  the mount point\u0027s inode, as on the backend.\n- Ceph, Gluster, RGW, KVSFS, SAUNAFS, LizardFS and MEM don\u0027t cross into other\n  file systems within an export, so fileid is already right for them.\n  PROXY_V3 has nothing to pass on, as NFSv3 has no mounted_on_fileid.\n\nmounted_on_fileid stays advertised for every FSAL because Ganesha can always\nanswer it correctly: RFC 8881 5.8.2.23 says it equals fileid except at the\nroot of a mounted file system, Ganesha handles the export root itself, and\nonly FSALs that have file systems mounted inside an export need to supply\nit, which VFS, XFS, GPFS and PROXY_V4 now do.\n\nBest wishes,\n\nEvan",
